 Notice that:\n\n- $\\frac{1}{n-1}{Σ}^{T}Σ$ is a diagonal $3\\times 3$ matrix, where the entries on the main diagonal are the eigenvalues (written in descending order) of the covariance matrix $C$, and\n\n- $V$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix, whose columns are the corresponding unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C.$\n\nTherefore, the columns of $V$ are the principal components of the data.\nSo, the first principal component is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{5}{3\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537969 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bd13cf7-7215-5f4f-8a7c-38c0559334ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537244} {:answer/id #uuid "8eb969dc-7431-591a-8333-b0ef3b0cc412" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537500} {:answer/id #uuid "ae62e6b6-9922-57f6-be87-76d55ca80e65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537559} {:answer/id #uuid "b1a7b6ab-39e1-5f20-9b17-720ce6944c95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537573} {:answer/id #uuid "f890beb3-8209-55e8-a5fa-269d219fee48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537738}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537244} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fdc00ed-8996-53bf-b0b6-f7702bef817b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537812}] :question/id #uuid "cd69e9f7-e929-5fe2-b3bb-4bb36c948590" :question/math-academy-id "q-345553" :question/problem "$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $U$ and $V$ above. Let $X$ be a $2\\times 3$ standardized observation matrix, where each row represents one observation and each column represents one feature. Given that $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $X,$ what is the third principal component of the data?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>{\\lambda }_{3}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${v}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${v}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- the $3$rd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ corresponding to the $3$rd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}.$\n\nNotice that the covariance matrix corresponding to $X$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{n-1}{X}^{T}X \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}(UΣ{V}^{T})^{T}(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}V{Σ}^{T}({U}^{T}U)Σ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V(\\frac{1}{n-1}{Σ}^{T}Σ){V}^{T},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the final expression represents the diagonal form of the covariance matrix $C.$ Notice that:\n\n- $\\frac{1}{n-1}{Σ}^{T}Σ$ is a diagonal $3\\times 3$ matrix, where the entries on the main diagonal are the eigenvalues (written in descending order) of the covariance matrix $C$, and\n\n- $V$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix, whose columns are the corresponding unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C.$\n\nTherefore, the columns of $V$ are the principal components of the data.\nSo, the third principal component is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537978 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07b599b3-e5fb-55be-8b4a-64cf19160660" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537238} {:answer/id #uuid "89ae6ffb-c4b7-5567-8681-07205f0f211d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{2}{v}_{2}^{T}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537491} {:answer/id #uuid "c54575f9-512e-55ef-a0c7-e74be9d25000" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{2}^{T}{v}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537610} {:answer/id #uuid "c6427590-8b6b-5345-bd73-92d79f31db5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537613} {:answer/id #uuid "feb49a45-3299-531f-989b-c646c0d9773a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537752}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537752} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a0ee814-fca1-58c8-8cc1-dbfe10fc9460" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537778}] :question/id #uuid "dcb3950f-e511-5b2b-8452-0fa93d40b2ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-345562" :question/problem "$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}| &  & | \\\\ {u}_{1} & ⋯ & {u}_{n} \\\\ | &  & |\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}| &  & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & ⋯ & {v}_{m} \\\\ | &  & |\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $U$ and $V$ above. Let $X$ be an $n\\times m$ standardized observation matrix, where each row represents one observation and each column represents one feature. Given that $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $X,$ what is the second principal component of the data?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${v}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${v}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nNotice that the covariance matrix corresponding to $X$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{n-1}{X}^{T}X \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}(UΣ{V}^{T})^{T}(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}V{Σ}^{T}({U}^{T}U)Σ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V(\\frac{1}{n-1}{Σ}^{T}Σ){V}^{T},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the final expression represents the diagonal form of the covariance matrix $C.$ Notice that:\n\n- $\\frac{1}{n-1}{Σ}^{T}Σ$ is a diagonal $m\\times m$ matrix, where the entries on the main diagonal are the eigenvalues (written in descending order) of the covariance matrix $C$, and\n\n- $V$ is a $m\\times m$ matrix, whose columns are the corresponding unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C.$\n\nTherefore, the columns of $V$ are the principal components of the data.\nSo, the second principal component is ${v}_{2}.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Principal Component from SVD"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192833 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192613 :question/id #uuid "ad59f3c2-8ca8-5d05-b626-501682800f01" :question/math-academy-id "e-21884" :question/problem "A financial analyst measured $5$ features, $x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_5,$ for each company among a group of companies under study. The collected data was stored in the standardized observation matrix $X,$ where each row contains measurements for a particular company.\n\nThe singular values of $X$ are \n\n$$\n\\sigma_1=7, \\quad \\sigma_2=5, \\quad \\sigma_3=3, \\quad \\sigma_4=2, \\quad \\sigma_5=1.\n$$\n\nThe analyst then converted the original observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ using the equation $Y = X V_2,$ where $V_2$ is the $5 \\times 2$ matrix consisting of the first two principal components of the data. What percentage of the original variance is preserved in the new transformed matrix $Y$ compared to the initial observation matrix $X?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $V$ be the matrix of principal components. Then, applying the change of variables\n\n$$\nY = XV\n$$\n\nwe can convert our observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ of (linearly) transformed observations such that\n\n- the covariance matrix of $Y$ is diagonal (meaning that the new features $y_1,y_2,\\ldots,y_5$ are uncorrelated),\n\n- the features $y_1,y_2,\\ldots,y_5$ are arranged in order of decreasing variance, and\n\n- the squares of the singular values $\\sigma_1^2, \\sigma_2^2, \\ldots, \\sigma_5^2$ represent the variances in the respective features $y_1,y_2,\\ldots,y_5.$\n\nNow, the total variance over all features is proportional to the sum of the squared singular values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Total} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(7)^{2}+(5)^{2}+(3)^{2}+(2)^{2}+(1)^{2} \\\\  & =88\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the analyst used only the first two principal components, the remaining variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Remaining} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(7)^{2}+(5)^{2} \\\\  & =74.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our required percentage can be computed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{Remaining}}{\\text{Total}} & =\\frac{74}{88} \\\\  & ≈0.841 \\\\  & =84.1%.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9dbfeaac-fc49-543d-b2b0-673b7dbb05d7" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193316 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac7d70fa-bf88-5d8a-b589-211fc1508e78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "86.5%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191824} {:answer/id #uuid "f22fa831-0ffa-5b44-8d4d-6444a734e999" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "83.5%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191825} {:answer/id #uuid "229c730c-c45a-5588-82ae-49b26c273ecb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "89.9%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191826} {:answer/id #uuid "41964a93-85b9-57dd-b859-9b726749f309" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "91.8%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191827} {:answer/id #uuid "4b4abaff-9204-5ea0-a799-46b24af32d24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "92.4%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191828}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "89.9%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191826} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c783572-57af-5f59-b3ff-cca2d8bf3bb8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191823}] :question/id #uuid "edd41528-ab75-576f-8bc0-8fe5a9ab1f40" :question/math-academy-id "q-343713" :question/problem "A scientist measured $5$ particular features, ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{5},$ for each specimen among a group of animals in danger of extinction. The collected data was stored in the standardized observation matrix $X,$ where each row contains measurements for a particular specimen.\nThe singular values of $X$ are \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}=7,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}=2,\\,{\\sigma }_{3}=1,\\,{\\sigma }_{4}=0.6,\\,{\\sigma }_{5}=0.4.\n$$\n\nThe scientist then converted the original observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ using the equation $Y=X{V}_{1},$ where ${V}_{1}$ is the $5\\times 1$ matrix consisting of the first principal component of the data. What percentage of the original variance is preserved in the new transformed matrix $Y$ compared to the initial observation matrix $X?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $V$ be the matrix of principal components. Then, applying the change of variables\n\n$$\nY=XV\n$$\n\nwe can convert our observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ of (linearly) transformed observations such that\n\n- the covariance matrix of $Y$ is diagonal (meaning that the new features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{5}$ are uncorrelated),\n\n- the features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{5}$ are arranged in order of decreasing variance, and\n\n- the squares of the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2},…,{\\sigma }_{5}^{2}$ represent the variances in the respective features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{5}.$\n\nNow, the total variance over all features is proportional to the sum of the squared singular values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Total} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(7)^{2}+(2)^{2}+(1)^{2}+(0.6)^{2}+(0.4)^{2} \\\\  & =54.52\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the scientist used only the first principal component, the remaining variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Remaining} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(7)^{2} \\\\  & =49.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our required percentage can be computed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{Remaining}}{\\text{Total}} & =\\frac{49}{54.52} \\\\  & ≈0.899 \\\\  & =89.9%.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193317 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "929589f9-2c1b-52ff-83d3-fa8d2f2ee07c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "91.3%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191830} {:answer/id #uuid "b7c2a84c-85c4-59d7-962e-1ed8ea8a8e48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "87.4%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191831} {:answer/id #uuid "0dac9873-d06e-599b-b424-744747db4a4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "95.8%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191832} {:answer/id #uuid "43f7b588-fc22-5ed8-8e11-88e773839d49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "97.1%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191833} {:answer/id #uuid "61cec5a4-6e98-53d4-b55b-1952616525f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "79.0%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191834}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "95.8%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191832} :answer-field/id #uuid "705a4ca2-8121-50c8-969c-f904844cb959" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191829}] :question/id #uuid "cd0aa760-2231-5a4e-8f82-cb5ce0612345" :question/math-academy-id "q-345593" :question/problem "A scientist measured $5$ particular features, ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{5},$ for each athlete among a group of athletes. The collected data was stored in the standardized observation matrix $X,$ where each row contains measurements for a particular athlete.\nThe singular values of $X$ are \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}=5,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}=2,\\,{\\sigma }_{3}=1,\\,{\\sigma }_{4}=0.5,\\,{\\sigma }_{5}=0.1.\n$$\n\nThe scientist then converted the original observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ using the equation $Y=X{V}_{2},$ where ${V}_{2}$ is the $5\\times 2$ matrix consisting of the first two principal components of the data. What percentage of the original variance is preserved in the new transformed matrix $Y$ compared to the initial observation matrix $X?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $V$ be the matrix of principal components. Then, applying the change of variables\n\n$$\nY=XV\n$$\n\nwe can convert our observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ of (linearly) transformed observations such that\n\n- the covariance matrix of $Y$ is diagonal (meaning that the new features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{5}$ are uncorrelated),\n\n- the features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{5}$ are arranged in order of decreasing variance, and\n\n- the squares of the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2},…,{\\sigma }_{5}^{2}$ represent the variances in the respective features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{5}.$\n\nNow, the total variance over all features is proportional to the sum of the squared singular values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Total} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(5)^{2}+(2)^{2}+(1)^{2}+(0.5)^{2}+(0.1)^{2} \\\\  & =30.26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the scientist used only the first two principal components, the remaining variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Remaining} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(5)^{2}+(2)^{2} \\\\  & =29.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our required percentage can be computed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{Remaining}}{\\text{Total}} & =\\frac{29}{30.26} \\\\  & ≈0.958 \\\\  & =95.8%.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525991 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16c0350c-0471-55de-ac70-f2529da87d8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "85.3%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525251} {:answer/id #uuid "1bed26d7-5712-5fad-9a47-4f08bb3eb187" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "87.4%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525265} {:answer/id #uuid "33b6e47c-35b6-5317-94f8-1bf5ec2d2141" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "84.7%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525335} {:answer/id #uuid "544af06f-53bf-5a45-9d45-b81c4c92cd56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "89.5%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525425} {:answer/id #uuid "9042a2f9-0756-5b9d-ad5e-9f3310959eb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "83.5%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525571}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "85.3%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525251} :answer-field/id #uuid "04e2e485-a811-5a11-9f6d-621be11e5c98" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525849}] :question/id #uuid "1b0e0b9e-9780-58a6-9804-62e273336ea3" :question/math-academy-id "q-345598" :question/problem "A water pollution researcher measured $4$ particular features, ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4},$ for each water sample in a region with suspicions of water pollution. The collected data was stored in the standardized observation matrix $X,$ where each row contains measurements for a particular sample.\nThe singular values of $X$ are \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}=9,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}=3,\\,{\\sigma }_{3}=2,\\,{\\sigma }_{4}=1.\n$$\n\nThe researcher then converted the original observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ using the equation $Y=X{V}_{1},$ where ${V}_{1}$ is the $4\\times 1$ matrix consisting of the first principal component of the data. What percentage of the original variance is preserved in the new transformed matrix $Y$ compared to the initial observation matrix $X?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $V$ be the matrix of principal components. Then, applying the change of variables\n\n$$\nY=XV\n$$\n\nwe can convert our observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ of (linearly) transformed observations such that\n\n- the covariance matrix of $Y$ is diagonal (meaning that the new features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{4}$ are uncorrelated),\n\n- the features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},{y}_{4}$ are arranged in order of decreasing variance, and\n\n- the squares of the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2},{\\sigma }_{3}^{2},{\\sigma }_{4}^{2}$ represent the variances in the respective features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},{y}_{4}.$\n\nNow, the total variance over all features is proportional to the sum of the squared singular values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Total} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(9)^{2}+(3)^{2}+(2)^{2}+(1)^{2} \\\\  & =95\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  researcher used only the first principal component, the remaining variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Remaining} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(9)^{2} \\\\  & =81.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our required percentage can be computed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{Remaining}}{\\text{Total}} & =\\frac{81}{95} \\\\  & ≈0.853 \\\\  & =85.3%.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526020 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01d7962d-301d-5564-b84c-5c4c275c4f5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "92.2%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525214} {:answer/id #uuid "2101ed94-0565-58d9-96d4-55fa409f7603" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90.8%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525280} {:answer/id #uuid "62e6b56c-6deb-528d-b4f2-3731cf383635" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "94.2%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525455} {:answer/id #uuid "d844e003-3bf2-5bc1-b18b-41cf1342c36c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "96.0%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525743} {:answer/id #uuid "f856d479-2998-5675-8c7e-c2b51e51ddc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "85.9%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525821}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90.8%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525280} :answer-field/id #uuid "2547a1a4-4d8c-5e2b-a028-871e88aaff4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525862}] :question/id #uuid "544f3b17-0710-5a62-83f3-a61ed0f7bcdd" :question/math-academy-id "q-345600" :question/problem "A quality inspector measured $5$ particular features, ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{5},$ for each machine in a factory. The collected data was stored in the standardized observation matrix $X,$ where each row contains measurements for a particular machine.\nThe singular values of $X$ are \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}=6,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}=4,\\,{\\sigma }_{3}=2,\\,{\\sigma }_{4}=1,\\,{\\sigma }_{5}=0.5.\n$$\n\nThe inspector then converted the original observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ using the equation $Y=X{V}_{2},$ where ${V}_{2}$ is the $5\\times 2$ matrix consisting of the first two principal components of the data. What percentage of the original variance is preserved in the new transformed matrix $Y$ compared to the initial observation matrix $X?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $V$ be the matrix of principal components. Then, applying the change of variables\n\n$$\nY=XV\n$$\n\nwe can convert our observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ of (linearly) transformed observations such that\n\n- the covariance matrix of $Y$ is diagonal (meaning that the new features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{5}$ are uncorrelated),\n\n- the features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{5}$ are arranged in order of decreasing variance, and\n\n- the squares of the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2},…,{\\sigma }_{5}^{2}$ represent the variances in the respective features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{5}.$\n\nNow, the total variance over all features is proportional to the sum of the squared singular values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Total} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(6)^{2}+(4)^{2}+(2)^{2}+(1)^{2}+(0.5)^{2} \\\\  & =57.25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the inspector used only the first two principal components, the remaining variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Remaining} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(6)^{2}+(4)^{2} \\\\  & =52.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our required percentage can be computed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{Remaining}}{\\text{Total}} & =\\frac{52}{57.25} \\\\  & ≈0.908 \\\\  & =90.8%.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526045 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20a97efd-6507-568d-a733-df35803cdd38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "84.9%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525279} {:answer/id #uuid "37741afd-0543-580c-8a1e-3d6da126b644" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "86.7%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525342} {:answer/id #uuid "b05f07be-33d9-5fa6-b04b-06111535bbdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "91.7%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525653} {:answer/id #uuid "da8a6c89-592b-5d39-adbd-d1d8791272ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "89.4%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525747} {:answer/id #uuid "dab33267-108b-5e93-b7c9-075b6c4a7248" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "87.6%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525750}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "89.4%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525747} :answer-field/id #uuid "337c107f-d7b5-5336-9dff-a1373b9e6812" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525873}] :question/id #uuid "7dcc2367-ae8e-5075-82b4-bcd5d8410a2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-345605" :question/problem "A nutritionist measured $5$ particular features, ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{5},$ for each client in a group under a specific diet. The collected data was stored in the standardized observation matrix $X,$ where each row contains measurements for a particular client.\nThe singular values of $X$ are \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}=6,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}=3,\\,{\\sigma }_{3}=2,\\,{\\sigma }_{4}=1,\\,{\\sigma }_{5}=0.6.\n$$\n\nThe nutritionist then converted the original observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ using the equation $Y=X{V}_{2},$ where ${V}_{2}$ is the $5\\times 2$ matrix consisting of the first two principal components of the data. What percentage of the original variance is preserved in the new transformed matrix $Y$ compared to the initial observation matrix $X?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $V$ be the matrix of principal components. Then, applying the change of variables\n\n$$\nY=XV\n$$\n\nwe can convert our observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ of (linearly) transformed observations such that\n\n- the covariance matrix of $Y$ is diagonal (meaning that the new features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{5}$ are uncorrelated),\n\n- the features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{5}$ are arranged in order of decreasing variance, and\n\n- the squares of the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2},…,{\\sigma }_{5}^{2}$ represent the variances in the respective features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{5}.$\n\nNow, the total variance over all features is proportional to the sum of the squared singular values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Total} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(6)^{2}+(3)^{2}+(2)^{2}+(1)^{2}+(0.6)^{2} \\\\  & =50.36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the nutritionist used only the first two principal components, the remaining variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Remaining} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(6)^{2}+(3)^{2} \\\\  & =45.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our required percentage can be computed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{Remaining}}{\\text{Total}} & =\\frac{45}{50.36} \\\\  & ≈0.894 \\\\  & =89.4%.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526092 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "516d873e-ccd4-5077-a4ce-d8e999a1ad90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "76.4%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525417} {:answer/id #uuid "7373c834-8f8c-55c5-9e2e-b8721d241460" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "68.9%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525499} {:answer/id #uuid "8d89f02b-374b-52f6-a7e9-61fe7c34455e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "62.8%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525559} {:answer/id #uuid "8fc471c5-7d2f-5671-88e6-6184f6fbad95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "66.8%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525568} {:answer/id #uuid "ef4774cd-feeb-566a-8ec8-b2c92c225f3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "78.7%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525800}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "68.9%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525499} :answer-field/id #uuid "266486de-9240-55f9-95f5-d5273efe84c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525864}] :question/id #uuid "e91cdcf4-5450-5f10-aac1-2b321d212e78" :question/math-academy-id "q-345607" :question/problem "A pharmacologist measured $5$ particular features, ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{5},$ for each drug among a group of potential treatments for high blood pressure. The collected data was stored in the standardized observation matrix $X,$ where each row contains measurements for a particular drug.\nThe singular values of $X$ are \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}=6,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}=3,\\,{\\sigma }_{3}=2,\\,{\\sigma }_{4}=1.5,\\,{\\sigma }_{5}=1.\n$$\n\nThe pharmacologist then converted the original observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ using the equation $Y=X{V}_{1},$ where ${V}_{1}$ is the $5\\times 1$ matrix consisting of the first principal component of the data. What percentage of the original variance is preserved in the new transformed matrix $Y$ compared to the initial observation matrix $X?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $V$ be the matrix of principal components. Then, applying the change of variables\n\n$$\nY=XV\n$$\n\nwe can convert our observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ of (linearly) transformed observations such that\n\n- the covariance matrix of $Y$ is diagonal (meaning that the new features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{5}$ are uncorrelated),\n\n- the features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{5}$ are arranged in order of decreasing variance, and\n\n- the squares of the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2},…,{\\sigma }_{5}^{2}$ represent the variances in the respective features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{5}.$\n\nNow, the total variance over all features is proportional to the sum of the squared singular values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Total} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(6)^{2}+(3)^{2}+(2)^{2}+(1.5)^{2}+(1)^{2} \\\\  & =52.25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the researcher used only the first principal component, the remaining variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Remaining} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(6)^{2} \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our required percentage can be computed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{Remaining}}{\\text{Total}} & =\\frac{36}{52.25} \\\\  & ≈0.689 \\\\  & =68.9%.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537922 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a343a9c-47b7-5434-8cc2-55123caf0ea0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "70.7%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537314} {:answer/id #uuid "3c0ed494-ccbd-5fee-87bf-c4772b8cd862" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "71.6%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537352} {:answer/id #uuid "8458816f-8b48-5cde-9d4a-33d4e2eff2af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "70.9%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537484} {:answer/id #uuid "f0ab9777-fef9-5b5d-be92-67582e5fc5cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "69.7%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537725} {:answer/id #uuid "fa0758bb-92c5-56b2-82ce-f22f66450474" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72.7%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537745}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "70.9%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537484} :answer-field/id #uuid "332d328e-144d-5da5-910d-9c9b357e23b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537775}] :question/id #uuid "6923f3da-7346-5c88-a6fe-bd6d1873e88f" :question/math-academy-id "q-345609" :question/problem "An economist measured $4$ particular features, ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4},$ for each currency in a collection under devaluation. The collected data was stored in the standardized observation matrix $X,$ where each row contains measurements for a particular currency.\nThe singular values of $X$ are \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}=12,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}=7,\\,{\\sigma }_{3}=3,\\,{\\sigma }_{4}=1.\n$$\n\nThe economist then converted the original observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ using the equation $Y=X{V}_{1},$ where ${V}_{1}$ is the $4\\times 1$ matrix consisting of the first principal component of the data. What percentage of the original variance is preserved in the new transformed matrix $Y$ compared to the initial observation matrix $X?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $V$ be the matrix of principal components. Then, applying the change of variables\n\n$$\nY=XV\n$$\n\nwe can convert our observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ of (linearly) transformed observations such that\n\n- the covariance matrix of $Y$ is diagonal (meaning that the new features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{4}$ are uncorrelated),\n\n- the features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},{y}_{4}$ are arranged in order of decreasing variance, and\n\n- the squares of the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2},{\\sigma }_{3}^{2},{\\sigma }_{4}^{2}$ represent the variances in the respective features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},{y}_{4}.$\n\nNow, the total variance over all features is proportional to the sum of the squared singular values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Total} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(12)^{2}+(7)^{2}+(3)^{2}+(1)^{2} \\\\  & =203\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the economist used only the first principal component, the remaining variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Remaining} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(12)^{2} \\\\  & =144.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our required percentage can be computed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{Remaining}}{\\text{Total}} & =\\frac{144}{203} \\\\  & ≈0.709 \\\\  & =70.9%.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537951 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49c18a90-d0ab-5db3-907e-9d6d19ce5251" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "58.3%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537385} {:answer/id #uuid "534fc293-1b38-5991-9c40-35df9573a0ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "63.1%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537402} {:answer/id #uuid "a003e2d0-0b5a-564f-bbea-c1c7cd17d282" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "57.1%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537528} {:answer/id #uuid "dccecae2-fe53-5ef0-8dd7-5f7144995552" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60.8%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537671} {:answer/id #uuid "f21bf8a8-668c-531d-a786-a924eb2ffa9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "71.2%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537728}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60.8%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537671} :answer-field/id #uuid "f239ec3f-54ad-5f78-85ed-e887372ab958" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537872}] :question/id #uuid "a62d30b2-87c9-5394-8f0b-7a8e71495548" :question/math-academy-id "q-345594" :question/problem "An epidemiologist measured $4$ particular features, ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4},$ for each disease affecting a specific population. The collected data was stored in the standardized observation matrix $X,$ where each row contains measurements for a particular individual.\nThe singular values of $X$ are \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}=11,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}=7,\\,{\\sigma }_{3}=5,\\,{\\sigma }_{4}=2.\n$$\n\nThe epidemiologist then converted the original observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ using the equation $Y=X{V}_{1},$ where ${V}_{1}$ is the $4\\times 1$ matrix consisting of the first principal component of the data. What percentage of the original variance is preserved in the new transformed matrix $Y$ compared to the initial observation matrix $X?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $V$ be the matrix of principal components. Then, applying the change of variables\n\n$$\nY=XV\n$$\n\nwe can convert our observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ of (linearly) transformed observations such that\n\n- the covariance matrix of $Y$ is diagonal (meaning that the new features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{4}$ are uncorrelated),\n\n- the features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},{y}_{4}$ are arranged in order of decreasing variance, and\n\n- the squares of the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2},{\\sigma }_{3}^{2},{\\sigma }_{4}^{2}$ represent the variances in the respective features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},{y}_{4}.$\n\nNow, the total variance over all features is proportional to the sum of the squared singular values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Total} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(11)^{2}+(7)^{2}+(5)^{2}+(2)^{2} \\\\  & =199\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the epidemiologist used only the first principal component, the remaining variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Remaining} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(11)^{2} \\\\  & =121.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our required percentage can be computed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{Remaining}}{\\text{Total}} & =\\frac{121}{199} \\\\  & ≈0.608 \\\\  & =60.8%.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Variance from SVD"}] :topic/math-academy-id 3946 :topic/title "The Connection Between PCA and SVD"}] [{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093060} :activity/id #uuid "2179f3df-5754-599f-a160-da910b72c2ff" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093058 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093431 :tutorial/content "Consider once again the standard basis ![](images/80/80dd67cd707fb8f6430942bd565fd4da3d7690b9013aa0813475caa3afcfe12b.svg) of the Euclidean plane ![](images/8b/8b0d045a28e5e130e31d4c31b5b7a36f3c46dc9fccfa55c2a73cba7e04a5951c.svg) and the unit square that is spanned by these vectors.\n\nLet's define a linear transformation ![](images/5a/5ab4d8d28ee7c7e99d35b1f9932f168e9311ecc0ca1dc0e494fdf73fa1baf484.svg) by its standard matrix ![](images/c2/c2c85081d1f89d74b8ebb2761496d211d7052193809b7864e7983437f5d0b80f.svg) given by \n\n![](images/8f/8fca8cf6dd5b232d3aed6eeb98fb6c244872c55425673cf3ac1441a22e2e29f3.svg) \n\nAs before, we can find the images of ![](images/a7/a7655c84c060c79a0c0850d5d0537e4b4d7b7826d0339e261f87aa7ed4f09229.svg) under ![](images/5a/5ab4d8d28ee7c7e99d35b1f9932f168e9311ecc0ca1dc0e494fdf73fa1baf484.svg) as follows:\n![](images/3f/3f8b5f0f827d359f32a4311021dc48947447133918d48a2f1cad511531b13c81.svg)\n\nPlotting the vectors ![](images/1f/1f249f835230ce9561402247b4c94e15cae46adc5e7dc4263316da3bfcf2348a.svg) and ![](images/bc/bcfcd6164292fee1aeff0dc8325607fc44ff8fe3fa41d4d3abfb95c17d98db92.svg) and their images ![](images/e5/e5f0a5e6b245b18ea6c8d6f65c82702cba528e740aacd5e97535e5705cea9415.svg) and ![](images/98/982d513f1246c98c5055421fad26619b5fe53085da40a017673554f8c992631d.svg) we obtain the following:\n\n![](images/41/4195c8cd2f22fb0f84056226fb7b28c5f9944f747ca3230bf187752829b2a04e.png)\n\nWe see that the transformation ![](images/5a/5ab4d8d28ee7c7e99d35b1f9932f168e9311ecc0ca1dc0e494fdf73fa1baf484.svg) represented by the matrix ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) defines a **counterclockwise rotation of ![](images/a0/a0497a2da4d79804cefd03dfabdca0316f86b4c119506a5afdc57c5e250b3adc.svg) or ![](images/87/873f07caba734ac094497c48f87df79ebf63194809a956ecd0e747bb8b593c39.svg) radians, about the origin.**\n\nThe transformation ![](images/5a/5ab4d8d28ee7c7e99d35b1f9932f168e9311ecc0ca1dc0e494fdf73fa1baf484.svg) can also be considered a rotation of ![](images/9d/9daf99b64dcaee73aea5338e639cab6c5c564b5975c3fe5eb525f082e4d660f5.svg) or ![](images/e0/e067125c7fb9ca2d725c3513586ec3ba8572b95b5dfac530bd8d35d116ac2ab0.svg) radians, *clockwise* about the origin." :tutorial/id #uuid "70a75b2e-3fa9-5aa9-b308-79b56d57fae7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3542 :tutorial/title "Rotations of 90 Degrees"} :step/id #uuid "f91bce30-93ef-5342-8485-0619a059a2b3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093062}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093059 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092223 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b86558a-4c99-5ccb-a100-719692a056f4"} :step/id #uuid "7452acd6-f07a-5cbb-a6a1-887429d397a8"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093060 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093432 :tutorial/content "Consider the standard basis ![](images/80/80dd67cd707fb8f6430942bd565fd4da3d7690b9013aa0813475caa3afcfe12b.svg) of the Euclidean plane ![](images/8b/8b0d045a28e5e130e31d4c31b5b7a36f3c46dc9fccfa55c2a73cba7e04a5951c.svg) and the unit square spanned by these two vectors. \n\nLet's define a linear transformation ![](images/5a/5ab4d8d28ee7c7e99d35b1f9932f168e9311ecc0ca1dc0e494fdf73fa1baf484.svg) by its standard matrix ![](images/c2/c2c85081d1f89d74b8ebb2761496d211d7052193809b7864e7983437f5d0b80f.svg) given by \n\n![](images/c6/c6865c5373231451885b3d76e3c8151e9c707462a8b405d9b014b1bda17065a8.svg)\n\nWe can find the images of ![](images/a7/a7655c84c060c79a0c0850d5d0537e4b4d7b7826d0339e261f87aa7ed4f09229.svg) under ![](images/5a/5ab4d8d28ee7c7e99d35b1f9932f168e9311ecc0ca1dc0e494fdf73fa1baf484.svg) by computing the matrix products, as follows:\n![](images/5b/5b8e1c8e03ebb60716eaa2fefded5e7fc286f6de9cffdece8200f6f56c2bbe8e.svg)\n\nPlotting the vectors ![](images/1f/1f249f835230ce9561402247b4c94e15cae46adc5e7dc4263316da3bfcf2348a.svg) and ![](images/bc/bcfcd6164292fee1aeff0dc8325607fc44ff8fe3fa41d4d3abfb95c17d98db92.svg) and their images ![](images/e5/e5f0a5e6b245b18ea6c8d6f65c82702cba528e740aacd5e97535e5705cea9415.svg) and ![](images/98/982d513f1246c98c5055421fad26619b5fe53085da40a017673554f8c992631d.svg) we obtain the following:\n\n![](images/c2/c2dc9f76bb23225cec0a4f62541d8a74c4c4b16d2e2f7c37e8d36922d97c59f0.png)\n\nWe see that the transformation ![](images/5a/5ab4d8d28ee7c7e99d35b1f9932f168e9311ecc0ca1dc0e494fdf73fa1baf484.svg) represented by the matrix ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) defines a **counterclockwise rotation of ![](images/a3/a3bf307d258c37da47916be4e50553ef612072912cd6ed8dc24fddafc6c3722a.svg) (or ![](images/86/8662109ab709179527b5d1768c0f42a8fb4d6493e41082c5317be360ec4169dd.svg) radians) about the origin.**\n\nNote that we typically refer to rotations in the counterclockwise sense. However, in this case, the transformation ![](images/5a/5ab4d8d28ee7c7e99d35b1f9932f168e9311ecc0ca1dc0e494fdf73fa1baf484.svg) can also be considered a rotation of ![](images/a3/a3bf307d258c37da47916be4e50553ef612072912cd6ed8dc24fddafc6c3722a.svg) (or ![](images/86/8662109ab709179527b5d1768c0f42a8fb4d6493e41082c5317be360ec4169dd.svg) radians) *clockwise* about the origin." :tutorial/id #uuid "410fc9b7-ab2d-52ba-9b26-7afd34fdf9e5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3545 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "68d9dc46-2592-5260-a1bb-53e6b885e53f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093061}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093061 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092224 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20f4ef93-3e7b-58ec-992e-3a62b12b74e2"} :step/id #uuid "9915017c-1452-5136-bb94-65860a727a47" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093058}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093062 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093434 :tutorial/content "Finally, let \n\n![](images/1b/1b840cbdb5b3953dfef9910d34248ea280bf38299e4f0a3d0a78143e7301f159.svg)\n\nbe a matrix of a linear transformation ![](images/2d/2d90be63023ff97e21e1a97ccee6c387437ac2a99787278c6f71f37e3ac86850.svg) As usual, we can find the images of ![](images/a7/a7655c84c060c79a0c0850d5d0537e4b4d7b7826d0339e261f87aa7ed4f09229.svg) under ![](images/5a/5ab4d8d28ee7c7e99d35b1f9932f168e9311ecc0ca1dc0e494fdf73fa1baf484.svg) as follows:\n![](images/76/76e24611a2cd8a3d5d01fc0a04a9b7a3d0957db84dd1a45bc2f1f82492fb2efd.svg)\n\nPlotting the vectors ![](images/1f/1f249f835230ce9561402247b4c94e15cae46adc5e7dc4263316da3bfcf2348a.svg) and ![](images/bc/bcfcd6164292fee1aeff0dc8325607fc44ff8fe3fa41d4d3abfb95c17d98db92.svg) and their images ![](images/e5/e5f0a5e6b245b18ea6c8d6f65c82702cba528e740aacd5e97535e5705cea9415.svg) and ![](images/98/982d513f1246c98c5055421fad26619b5fe53085da40a017673554f8c992631d.svg) we obtain the following:\n\n![](images/0f/0fc5f33d0bd7047ab02bd9bc3ea3b00c083ab214f3bc8a51e9cbb6e54dc6b51b.png)\n\nWe see that the transformation ![](images/5a/5ab4d8d28ee7c7e99d35b1f9932f168e9311ecc0ca1dc0e494fdf73fa1baf484.svg) represented by the matrix ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) defines a **counterclockwise rotation of ![](images/9d/9daf99b64dcaee73aea5338e639cab6c5c564b5975c3fe5eb525f082e4d660f5.svg) or ![](images/e0/e067125c7fb9ca2d725c3513586ec3ba8572b95b5dfac530bd8d35d116ac2ab0.svg) radians, about the origin.**\n\nThe transformation ![](images/5a/5ab4d8d28ee7c7e99d35b1f9932f168e9311ecc0ca1dc0e494fdf73fa1baf484.svg) can also be considered a rotation of ![](images/a0/a0497a2da4d79804cefd03dfabdca0316f86b4c119506a5afdc57c5e250b3adc.svg) or ![](images/87/873f07caba734ac094497c48f87df79ebf63194809a956ecd0e747bb8b593c39.svg) radians *clockwise* about the origin." :tutorial/id #uuid "49541094-17cb-52e7-bb58-8ee2199ad6d6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3547 :tutorial/title "Rotations of 270 Degrees"} :step/id #uuid "d48d44d1-fe67-587c-a56e-251eee6b68f8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093135}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093135 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092274 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4df33ff-e04e-55ec-b273-9eceff7db53b"} :step/id #uuid "496d3d86-9264-56e9-a8a9-63d5cf7428bf" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093059}}] :activity/title "Right-Angle Rotations as Linear Transformations" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186092430}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186048712 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092223 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092065 :question/id #uuid "a74be952-4dd4-5a7f-a8a4-de68977c5bb1" :question/math-academy-id "e-5457" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at  $(-5,-1), (1,4), (3,-5).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of $S',$ the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation $\\mathbf{R}_{-90^\\circ}$ by an angle of $90^\\circ$ about the origin?\n\n- $(1,-5)$\n\n- $(4,-1)$\n\n- $(-5,-3)$" :question/worked-solution "The clockwise rotation $\\mathbf{R}_{-90^\\circ}$ by an angle of $90^\\circ$ is the same as counterclockwise rotation by an angle of $270^\\circ.$ It's given by the matrix\n\n![](images/7f/7f8b60362b08e038452b3454b1719c6f51df7d0129cc7d957e328a92b4f7e7c3.svg)\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of $\\mathbf{R}_{-90^\\circ},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S$ as columns:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $\\mathbf{R}_{-90^\\circ}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 & -5 \\\\ 5 & -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of $S'$ are $(-1,5), (4,-1), (-5,-3).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b86558a-4c99-5ccb-a100-719692a056f4" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092520 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c033e18-ddee-5947-8736-015fb0b944bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459461} {:answer/id #uuid "4b27be02-c7df-5731-b2ab-e94327db3b43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459462}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459462} :answer-field/id #uuid "9caa6409-86f1-595b-bb06-d1f4e6c47bb3" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460597} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c45483a-9d1b-5943-ba0a-df360705b502" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459463} {:answer/id #uuid "89499bb9-7c26-57c0-a190-7fb076f6c96a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459464}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459464} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d8a4422-c150-5766-aec8-3e169e385793" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460598} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ed5c77b-8f63-52b4-8da2-fdc13fe86ba7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459465} {:answer/id #uuid "929dbdfb-c1e2-55a2-b259-4219c1aceaf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459466}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459465} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5a5232a-90b4-56c2-a20e-c9c94d5493cf" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460599}] :question/id #uuid "affd1161-3d1a-5157-8f5d-ad61f32ab379" :question/math-academy-id "q-12126" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at  $(-2,-3),(-3,2),(2,4).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation ${R}_{-{90}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${90}^{∘}$ about the origin?\n- **I.** $(-3,2)$\n- **II.** $(4,-2)$\n- **III.** $(-4,2)$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The clockwise rotation ${R}_{-{90}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${90}^{∘}$ is the same as counterclockwise rotation by an angle of ${270}^{∘}.$ It's given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{-{90}^{∘}},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{-{90}^{∘}}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(-3,2),(2,3),(4,-2).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092521 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67eeda6f-1b61-5741-8a5d-e1f43238a6e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459467} {:answer/id #uuid "61785b41-e7ef-57ae-a54d-b73879f45e76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459468}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459467} :answer-field/id #uuid "25df9cd3-4cf6-5d06-abbd-9ecf000944d1" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460600} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9dfd49d5-6956-57ea-8c3f-0233cfc70c7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459469} {:answer/id #uuid "e95f29b2-ef74-51f6-9207-df19d9b00bc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459470}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459470} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ebf9e0c-03f7-59b2-85f7-834418b17034" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460601} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b0e1a33-8cfb-5faa-9096-1d3a4d8e4587" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459471} {:answer/id #uuid "6050f8c6-e524-5924-a4f1-6fa382f14808" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459472}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459472} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ba05e80-ac34-56c9-b8d5-b8c9de399ba0" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460602}] :question/id #uuid "4ff1deaa-4798-5008-88bc-6c10716485ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-12130" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-2,-1),(1,4),(1,-1).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{\\pi /2}$ by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians about the origin?\n- **I.** $(4,-2)$\n- **II.** $(1,-2)$\n- **III.** $(1,1)$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{\\pi /2}$ by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians is given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{\\pi /2},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{\\pi /2}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(1,-2),(-4,1),(1,1).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253586 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "932fd48b-8935-57c7-8293-eb9a71377d6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461869} {:answer/id #u