o} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394175}] :question/id #uuid "be344c11-b1a1-5df9-959f-34ab811cf29b" :question/math-academy-id "q-81450" :question/problem "![](images/d1/d111b1df75cfaa0d86d17c06584c57b19ca4711ae3e6720e68d1704fd72d86ae.png)\n\nConsider the vector $v$ shown above. Find the vector $Av$ given that \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Consider the standard basis ${i,j}$ of the Euclidean plane ${R}^{2}$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors. Computing the images of ${i,j}$ under $A$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =Ai=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=j \\\\ {j}^{′} & =Aj=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=-i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/f1/f154267ed0a528b1e9fbec40064b090560410fe76dab00070e13fb3d1978b59c.png)\n\nSo, the matrix represents a rotation of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians counterclockwise about the origin.\n\nTherefore, in order to find $Av$, we need to rotate $v$ counterclockwise by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians, as shown below.\n\n![](images/dd/dd0496914b35f969896c6a54907fbfa5ea6c139c878d8682b933a8757bde7524.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092828 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4ee5a39-1390-51c0-b274-4ee4180f4f8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/31/3137a6103d045a9a2ed37da248a60f0672adca738833c73ac7b4ba8bce735842.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091169} {:answer/id #uuid "f9855d31-e87b-5cbf-9c51-3a9333c5591d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/48/4821d747a025a0c6501f70d630d197d976fa1cfd743f96f16d4d42f440db105e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091170} {:answer/id #uuid "2700e5f7-a289-5dc7-afd5-ff540e82a1c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/