c35e2e740038264f2be05bffb90214249ec5602591623d457325db3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276611} {:answer/id #uuid "c89d8c25-ddbe-5e3d-9e44-1ee0e28ea7dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/5b/5bd4d8cc2643767b82acc0e61d168f5b387be67a7ff96634604e0d6828cc288b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277262}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ba/ba328088ca187ea28b241a302a486da1145a51c7e134d8d4f529f5f4a916ddc1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275520} :answer-field/id #uuid "7489f911-b3ed-5658-95a0-34d33dc19d87" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277931}] :question/id #uuid "ecda41b1-c814-51d0-b755-d1273a4b443b" :question/math-academy-id "q-160619" :question/problem "![](images/f3/f3e31d1c15a97adea32790eafe75ad1bc9f70c854b516b98de6648ec8d7bcb87.png)\n\nConsider the vector $v$ shown above. Find the vector $Av$ given that \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Consider the standard basis $\\{i,j\\}$ of the Euclidean plane ${R}^{2}$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors. Computing the images of $\\{i,j\\}$ under $A$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =A(i)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=-i \\\\ {j}^{′} & =A(j)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/9f/9fae3ec0ada3008937d88e4e76374a56da4369b1821cfee072c90d88b562df46.png)\n\nSo, the matrix represents a rotation of $\\pi $ radians (counterclockwise or clockwise) about the origin.\n\nTherefore, in order to find $Av$, we need to rotate $v$ (counterclockwise or clockwise) by an angle of $\\pi $ radians, as shown below.\n\n![](images/ae/ae800b271848948b