rked-solution "Consider the standard basis ${i,j}$ of the Euclidean plane ${R}^{2}$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors. Computing the images of ${i,j}$ under $A$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =A(i)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=-i \\\\ {j}^{′} & =A(j)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/9f/9fae3ec0ada3008937d88e4e76374a56da4369b1821cfee072c90d88b562df46.png)\n\nSo, the matrix represents a rotation of $\\pi $ radians (counterclockwise or clockwise) about the origin.\n\nTherefore, in order to find $Av$, we need to rotate $v$ (counterclockwise or clockwise) by an angle of $\\pi $ radians, as shown below.\n\n![](images/d0/d097103ae0d3a97657568a11df87c5422136c7d1ccdef7ea86e38703580b95a4.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278324 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03e1ef34-8623-5d0f-8ded-90bee0606743" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b2/b2beaf70e860c2e94f271cfb09b72aa3a256f0ec267ea85318329dd61ce9f44b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275514} {:answer/id #uuid "344aa95e-b395-5ce3-86a0-c800123488b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/23/23f63717c8d356c698137359824112511b0bbd5ec04c59e47d22500d842d03b6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275944} {:answer/id #uuid "56287947-d86a-5b54-ae46-0a3e43f0a8b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/90/9038b103842cd92233490de2306c804195675337f28011d8b93434f4f2813279.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276243} {:answer/id #uuid "6b718fdf-6d85-590d-9d90-b76057b0ae3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/cb/cbcb59ec78c02f46704f7d56421e4265bb1fa199ecdef6c798ddd4e5fd4349d8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276440} {:a