lockwise** about the origin.\n\nTherefore, in order to find $Av$, we need to rotate $v$ **clockwise** by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians, as shown below.\n\n![](images/ab/ab6a3ec35954243105f9bb6c9f1f2cc21c02358f90855f9f36f0a590092eb93d.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423032 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "288ae498-c828-5f25-a662-34bb3cc9fb83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3a/3a7e4f6e534ec77a08e425b86d97f43535c376909c80aa6621d0061ce1127b45.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422999} {:answer/id #uuid "81f1bfe5-7671-572d-9d6f-f45ae5bf628c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c9/c95c1f93ccc6c6c2ff7264ac5bca90680ff2c3c745d95acb0f81d288bf4e498a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423006} {:answer/id #uuid "9e496286-d2de-52ce-910a-7a26b9ff855c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e6/e6a72b4fb4a9c100996e7761407a2eb923d22f0d26832964bea2d4d6e3c90f2e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423009} {:answer/id #uuid "a02d8596-45e0-50ef-be7e-df004a71999d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d6/d64f49a072fd7841d1d1b81e396673f3addcfa05005128e969dbb6ea4697d358.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423010} {:answer/id #uuid "cd784227-675e-564f-b619-c32eee8716e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4f/4fd9da23db36708a9071fb73ba48743cc9720ae28355a8e84e39f07ff29e6bd4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423017}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c9/c95c1f93ccc6c6c2ff7264ac5bca90680ff2c3c745d95acb0f81d288bf4e498a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423006} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4e37cf0-5789-5204-8684-f5f020026bff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423028}] :question/id #uuid "c99e9b3e-fb97-5628-9428-4f4b221940ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-81826" :question/problem "![](images/f2/f2b74f78040024982099dac9b8670ef0f3fddd8bd5c88c3fa2b097659876c15a.png)\n\nConsider the vector $v$ shown above. Find the vector $Av$ given that \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Consider the standard basis $\\{i,j\\}$ of the Euclidean plane ${R}^{2}$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors. Computing the images of $\\{i,j\\}$ under $A$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =A(i)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=-i \\\\ {j}^{′} & =A(j)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/4e/4e0e5c9cee037bded1f5739af35c3a8acfaa4d4e6f469b3e50afe69b96990e39.png)\n\nSo, the matrix represents a rotation of $\\pi $ radians (counterclockwise or clockwise) about the origin.\n\nTherefore, in order to find $Av$, we need to rotate $v$ (counterclockwise or clockwise) by an angle of $\\pi $ radians, as shown below.\n\n![](images/bd/bdb71769b9035f137125e4242f9653ba9d346a4507042a8b864227e23498b781.png)"}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Rotation Matrices"}] :topic/math-academy-id 931 :topic/title "Right-Angle Rotations as Linear Transformations"}] [{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158091} :activity/id #uuid "f696f0f4-7c66-58b2-8b6c-9b62c56bed9c" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158091 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158432 :tutorial/content "We can extend a scalar function $f(x)$ to a square matrix argument $A.$ This function $f(A)$ is called a **matrix function**, and maps a square matrix to another square matrix of the same size.\n\nGiven a square matrix $A$ and a scalar function $f$ with Maclaurin series\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\sum_{k=0}^\\infty c_k x^k,\n$$\n\nwe can define $f(A)$ by replacing $x$ with $A,$ with addition and multiplication performed in the matrix sense:\n\n$$\nf(A) = \\sum_{k=0}^\\infty c_k A^k\n$$\n\nFor example, recall that the Maclaurin series of $f(x) = e^x$ is\n\n$$\ne^x = \\sum_{k=0}^\\infty \\dfrac{x^k}{k!}.\n$$\n\nThen, for a square matrix $A,$ we define the matrix exponential as\n\n$$\ne^A = \\sum_{k=0}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{k!}A^k.\n$$\n\nIn the next slide, we'll see how to apply matrix functions to square matrices." :tutorial/id #uuid "c3451824-f52b-539c-b16f-3819d3ea37df" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10688 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "215d5365-8c77-5734-bfe7-0ac93d99aa62" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158092}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158092 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158433 :tutorial/content "Suppose $D$ is a diagonal matrix with diagonal entries $\\lambda_1, \\ldots, \\lambda_n{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2} & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & 0 & ⋯ & {\\lambda }_{n}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFor a scalar function $f$ with a Maclaurin series expansion, $f(D)$ is the diagonal matrix with diagonal entries $f(\\lambda_1), \\ldots, f(\\lambda_n){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}f({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ 0 & f({\\lambda }_{2}) & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & 0 & ⋯ & f({\\lambda }_{n})\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFor example, given the matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand the function $f(x) = e^x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}f(2) & 0 \\\\ 0 & f(1)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis rule works because raising a diagonal matrix to a power is equivalent to raising each diagonal entry to the same power.\n\nIndeed, if\n$\\displaystyle\nf(x) = \\sum_{k=0}^\\infty c_k x^k$\nis the Maclaurin series of $f,$ then, by the definition of $f(D),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(D) & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{\\infty }{c}_{k}{D}^{k} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{\\infty }{c}_{k}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2} & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & 0 & ⋯ & {\\lambda }_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}^{k} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{\\infty }{c}_{k}\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1}^{k} & 0 & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}^{k} & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & 0 & ⋯ & {\\lambda }_{n}^{k}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\sum }_{k=0}^{\\infty }{c}_{k}{\\lambda }_{1}^{k} & 0 & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\sum }_{k=0}^{\\infty }{c}_{k}{\\lambda }_{2}^{k} & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & 0 & ⋯ & {\\sum }_{k=0}^{\\infty }{c}_{k}{\\lambda }_{n}^{k}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}f({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ 0 & f({\\lambda }_{2}) & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & 0 & ⋯ & f({\\lambda }_{n})\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "da162714-f641-5ded-aec8-6f24c0d5cbb3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10689 :tutorial/title "Function of a Diagonal Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "c8b4e298-ed67-50ea-bb52-8968e4cd58bf" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158093}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158093 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157268 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "343f8efc-fdef-51dc-ad3f-fad1ab37bc88"} :step/id #uuid "91d86e1a-5d29-5149-9978-86770afc6de0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158094}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158094 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158434 :tutorial/content "Suppose $J_n(\\lambda)$ is an $n\\times n$ Jordan block where the main diagonal entries are all $\\lambda{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\lambda  & 1 & 0 & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\lambda  & 1 & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & 0 & \\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & ⋯ & 0 & \\lambda \\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFor a scalar function $f$ analytic at $\\lambda$ (i.e., has a Taylor series expansion at $\\lambda$), $f(J_n(\\lambda))$ is the matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{'}(\\lambda ) & \\frac{{f}^{''}(\\lambda )}{2!} & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-1)}(\\lambda )}{(n-1)!} \\\\ 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{'}(\\lambda ) & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-2)}(\\lambda )}{(n-2)!} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{'}(\\lambda ) \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find $f(J_3(2))$ for $f(x) = \\ln x.$\n\nSince $J_3(2)$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix, we will need the first $3-1=2$ derivatives of $f{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}x \\\\ {f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {f}^{''}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n![](images/43/439d1a1c3c2c315510fd293e8bb8e1467e149222025359a0e0d899270eb07e12.svg)\n\nNow that we can compute the value of a function of a Jordan block, we can also find the value of a function of a Jordan matrix.\n\nThe matrix [math]f(J_n(\\lambda))[/math] for [math]n=2,3,[/math] and [math]4[/math] are as follows.\n\n* For [math]2\\times 2[/math] Jordan blocks, we have\n\\[\nf(J_2(\\lambda)) =\n\\begin{bmatrix}\n\tf(\\lambda) & f'(\\lambda) \\\\\n\t0 & f(\\lambda)\n\\end{bmatrix}.\n\\]\n\n* For [math]3\\times 3[/math] Jordan blocks, we have\n\\[\nf(J_3(\\lambda)) =\n\\begin{bmatrix}\n\tf(\\lambda) & f'(\\lambda) & \\dfrac12f''(\\lambda) \\\\\n\t0 & f(\\lambda) & f'(\\lambda) \\\\\n\t0 & 0 & f(\\lambda)\n\\end{bmatrix}.\n\\]\n\n* For [math]4\\times 4[/math] Jordan blocks, we have\n\\[\nf(J_4(\\lambda)) =\n\\begin{bmatrix}\n\tf(\\lambda) & f'(\\lambda) & \\dfrac12f''(\\lambda) & \\dfrac16f'''(\\lambda) \\\\\n\t0 & f(\\lambda) & f'(\\lambda) & \\dfrac12f''(\\lambda) \\\\\n\t0 & 0 & f(\\lambda) & f'(\\lambda) \\\\\n\t0 & 0 & 0 & f(\\lambda)\n\\end{bmatrix}.\n\\]" :tutorial/id #uuid "7972371e-9205-5e74-b277-6dfcab332f9b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10691 :tutorial/title "Function of a Jordan Block"} :step/id #uuid "deee50a7-b19c-5fb9-80a5-a024ec3943c0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158424}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158095 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157269 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8ce12d6f-8d1c-52f3-992f-534b9d91dda9"} :step/id #uuid "c9bdde30-6bf2-5ffc-93a3-92c41bf6792d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158096}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158096 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157270 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "87883002-67eb-5111-b15a-c5941bb33f9b"} :step/id #uuid "f4444ff2-15d1-524f-952e-a3223b79ead3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158097}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158097 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158435 :tutorial/content "Suppose $A$ and $S$ are matrices, where $S$ is invertible.\nFor a scalar function $f$ with a Maclaurin series expansion, we have\n\n$$\nf(SAS^{-1}) = Sf(A)S^{-1}.\n$$\n\nIndeed, given the Maclaurin series $\\displaystyle f(x) = \\sum_{k=0}^\\infty c_kx^k,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(SA{S}^{-1}) & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{\\infty }{c}_{k}(SA{S}^{-1})^{k} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{\\infty }{c}_{k}S{A}^{k}{S}^{-1} \\\\  & =S({\\sum }_{k=0}^{\\infty }{c}_{k}{A}^{k}){S}^{-1} \\\\  & =Sf(A){S}^{-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo far, we have seen how to evaluate functions for diagonal matrices and Jordan blocks. With the formula above, we will now be able to\n\n- evaluate $f(B)$ for diagonalizable $B$ using a diagonalization $B=PDP^{-1},$ and\n\n- evaluate $f(B)$ for non-diagonalizable $B$ using a Jordan decomposition $B=PJP^{-1}.$\n\nLet's see how with an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "4241af18-972e-530c-b06b-388437001b24" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10692 :tutorial/title "Function of a Jordan Decomposition"} :step/id #uuid "82e8fc5c-326f-538e-acdc-fbdce35a6bb4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158098}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158098 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157271 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "706303c1-0e4c-55f7-8b62-5094938f85bc"} :step/id #uuid "62349611-970a-5bd8-b8c7-3d773fb63239"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158424 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158466 :tutorial/content "Consider a Jordan matrix formed by the direct sum of blocks $J_{n_1}(\\lambda_1) {\\textstyle\\:\\oplus\\:} \\cdots {\\textstyle\\:\\oplus\\:} J_{n_k}(\\lambda_k)$ and a function $f.$ By applying the rule for diagonal matrices to this block diagonal matrix, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{{n}_{1}}({\\lambda }_{1})\\,⊕\\,⋯\\,⊕\\,{J}_{{n}_{k}}({\\lambda }_{k})) & =f(\\begin{bmatrix}{J}_{{n}_{1}}({\\lambda }_{1}) & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & {J}_{{n}_{k}}({\\lambda }_{k})\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}f({J}_{{n}_{1}}({\\lambda }_{1})) & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & f({J}_{{n}_{k}}({\\lambda }_{k}))\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =f({J}_{{n}_{1}}({\\lambda }_{1}))\\,⊕\\,⋯\\,⊕\\,f({J}_{{n}_{k}}({\\lambda }_{k})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis allows us to compute functions of any matrix in Jordan Normal Form by applying the function to each Jordan block individually.\n\nFor example, suppose $J = J_2(2) \\oplus J_1(3).$ Then, applying a function $f,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}f({J}_{2}(2)) & 0 \\\\ 0 & f({J}_{1}(3))\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e85abcea-5a44-50b9-9104-b71309622287" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14947 :tutorial/title "Function of a Jordan Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "b87874f9-2306-59b4-a907-b5f36d18a293" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158095}}] :activity/title "Matrix Functions" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186157520} {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414010} :activity/id #uuid "f7c04eb8-7724-552c-b148-1c56cf42932a" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414007 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414006 :question/id #uuid "da6ab59f-cfd7-5d6f-94dd-f3fddb0a05c8"} :step/id #uuid "67945b3f-2216-5dac-be50-954f07e6800a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414011}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414008 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414005 :question/id #uuid "7819c8fe-793f-5300-ba05-a00d3d72cc5c"} :step/id #uuid "7697fba1-0f90-5fc4-8a3d-0c36de70f971"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414009 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414003 :question/id #uuid "0a64ad91-1a79-5d6b-9232-aac7246f71fe"} :step/id #uuid "9a30b151-2968-59bd-b00e-dd670ecca6ba" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414007}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414010 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157694 :question/id #uuid "e45b0296-d991-5cc8-b0d3-09c2315c742d"} :step/id #uu