 $1$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({J}_{4}(1))=\\,\\,$ |  | $0$ | $0$ | {{field-3}} | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |\n|  | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | {{field-4}} |  |  |\n|  |  | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ |  |" :question/worked-solution "Given a function $f$ and a Jordan block ${J}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ we have\n\n$$\nf({J}_{n}(\\lambda ))=\\begin{bmatrix}f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & \\frac{{f}^{″}(\\lambda )}{2!} & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-1)}(\\lambda )}{(n-1)!} \\\\ 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-2)}(\\lambda )}{(n-2)!} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ $n=4,$ and $\\lambda =1.$\n\nSince $n=4,$ we need to compute the first $4-1=3$ derivatives of $f:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{4}(1)) & =\\begin{bmatrix}f(1) & {f}^{′}(1) & \\frac{1}{2!}{f}^{″}(1) & \\frac{1}{3!}{f}^{‴}(1) \\\\ 0 & f(1) & {f}^{′}(1) & \\frac{1}{2!}{f}^{″}(1) \\\\ 0 & 0 & f(1) & {f}^{′}(1) \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & f(1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1) & \\frac{1}{1} & -\\frac{1}{{1}^{2}\\cdot 2} & \\frac{2}{{1}^{3}\\cdot 6} \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{ln}⁡(1) & \\frac{1}{1} & -\\frac{1}{{1}^{2}\\cdot 2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\operatorname{ln}⁡(1) & \\frac{1}{1} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & \\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414003 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7017ca3d-0bfe-52d1-9050-c702cd83a55e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e