answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377023} :answer-field/id #uuid "8272e4c5-2429-505b-927b-2750e3b5d005" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377022}] :question/id #uuid "a1a8e997-11f9-5693-8fe0-4790fffa9c36" :question/math-academy-id "q-253879" :question/problem "Let ${J}_{n}(\\lambda )$ denote an $n\\times n$ Jordan block with $\\lambda $ on the main diagonal. Fill in the missing entries in the matrix below.\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} | $0$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\operatorname{cos}⁡({J}_{3}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(\\pi ))=\\,\\,$ |  | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | {{field-4}} | $0$ |  |\n|  | $0$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $0$ |  |  |\n|  |  | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | {{field-5}} |  |" :question/worked-solution "Given a function $f$ and a Jordan block ${J}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ we have\n\n$$\nf({J}_{n}(\\lambda ))=\\begin{bmatrix}f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & \\frac{{f}^{″}(\\lambda )}{2!} & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-1)}(\\lambda )}{(n-1)!} \\\\ 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-2)}(\\lambda )}{(n-2)!} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({J}_{3}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\begin{bmatrix}{J}_{3}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & 0 \\\\ 0 & {J}_{1}(\\pi )\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({J}_{3}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})) & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡({J}_{1}(\\pi ))\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡({J}_{3}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}))\\,⊕\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡({J}_{1}(\\pi )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the function of each Jordan block separately.\n\n- For ${J}_{3}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}),$ we have $n=3$ and $\\lambda =2.$ \\since $n=3,$ we need to compute the first and second derivatives of $f$ only:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{3}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})) & =\\begin{bmatrix}f(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & \\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\ 0 & f(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\ 0 & 0 & f(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & -\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} & -\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For ${J}_{1}(\\pi ),$ we have $n=1$ and $\\lambda =1.$ since $n=1,$ we don't need any derivatives. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{1}(\\pi )) & =\\begin{bmatrix}f(\\pi )\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({J}_{3}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(\\pi )) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡({J}_{3}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}))\\,⊕\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡({J}_{1}(\\pi )) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} & -\\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377024 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "047fb130-8e41-5582-9c42-b83a6bb1146b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377026}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377026} :answer-field/id #uuid "acae7b39-7953-5587-912c-6bed72a6c056" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377025} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70c17988-23a4-572d-8b74-6c4722e3c968" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377028}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377028} :answer-field/id #uuid "5705bbf5-4328-58b5-b584-6e1e7194f2ef" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377027} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0364ea93-0af0-54d2-be0c-5dfd79fa6aa1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377030}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377030} :answer-field/id #uuid "81e48ce1-6107-5825-801a-1e0617853f5e" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377029} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43b8a538-70dd-5e5b-b76e-8e96ca8ca561" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377032}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377032} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd78b681-ad83-5968-92bf-a31700f6b55b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377031} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a06eeb0-50da-58ee-8b6f-2f69fa647f2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377034}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377034} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce9e2d83-4b34-5dcb-8256-50eb7278faf4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377033}] :question/id #uuid "07f3343a-f24c-59e9-b03d-4ce328a45d84" :question/math-academy-id "q-253891" :question/problem "Let ${J}_{n}(\\lambda )$ denote an $n\\times n$ Jordan block with $\\lambda $ on the main diagonal. Fill in the missing entries in the matrix below.\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\operatorname{tan}⁡({J}_{1}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,⊕\\,{J}_{3}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))=\\,\\,$ |  | $0$ | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} | $2$ |  |\n|  | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | {{field-4}} |  |  |\n|  |  | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | {{field-5}} |  |" :question/worked-solution "Given a function $f$ and a Jordan block ${J}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ we have\n\n$$\nf({J}_{n}(\\lambda ))=\\begin{bmatrix}f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & \\frac{{f}^{″}(\\lambda )}{2!} & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-1)}(\\lambda )}{(n-1)!} \\\\ 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-2)}(\\lambda )}{(n-2)!} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({J}_{1}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,⊕\\,{J}_{3}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\begin{bmatrix}{J}_{1}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & 0 \\\\ 0 & {J}_{3}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({J}_{1}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})) & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{tan}⁡({J}_{3}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡({J}_{1}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}))\\,⊕\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡({J}_{3}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the function of each Jordan block separately.\n\n- For ${J}_{1}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}),$ we have $n=1$ and $\\lambda =3.$ since $n=1,$ we don't need any derivatives. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{1}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})) & =\\begin{bmatrix}f(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For ${J}_{3}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),$ we have $n=3$ and $\\lambda =2.$ \\since $n=3,$ we need to compute the first and second derivatives of $f$ only:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =2{sec}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{3}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})) & =\\begin{bmatrix}f(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & \\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ 0 & f(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ 0 & 0 & f(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & {sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & {sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({J}_{1}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,⊕\\,{J}_{3}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})) & =({J}_{1}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}))\\,⊕\\,({J}_{3}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414005 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bf34227-df92-59d8-9613-ceb5484789cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413956}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413956} :answer-field/id #uuid "25dde8d8-265c-54c9-ac3f-e40a69e95898" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592